## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about nuanced trade-offs in privacy-first on-device inference with mixed offload, model update cadence, and rollout under regulatory constraints across devices and regions.

## Key Concepts

- On-device causal inference DAG
- Edge vs cloud offload costs
- Privacy/compliance budgeting
- Model update strategy and drift
- Rollout planning and break-even

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost estimator
function estimateCBA(params){
  const {ramPerNode, latency, inferencesPerDay, offloadKbPerDay, cloudCostPerKb, energyPerInference} = params;
  const deviceCost = ramPerNode * 0.1 + energyPerInference * inferencesPerDay;
  const offloadCost = offloadKbPerDay * cloudCostPerKb;
  const privacyOverhead = 0.08;
  const total = deviceCost + offloadCost * (1 + privacyOverhead);
  return {total, privacyOverhead};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model drift costs and retraining triggers?
- What monitoring would you use to validate break-even?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates cost modeling for federated, multi-app ML with privacy constraints—a realistic production use case at companies like Google (Gboard), Apple, and Meta. It tests ability to identify cost drivers, trade-offs between privacy and accuracy, and phased rollout planning under regulatory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning with secure aggregation and differential privacy
- Cross-app governance, regional data locality (GDPR, CCPA, PIPL)
- Bandwidth, energy, and memory budgeting on mobile devices
- Model drift detection, concept drift, and maintenance pipelines
- Phased regional rollout with go/no-go gates

## Code Example
```javascript
function calculateCBAPhase(devices, roundsPerDay, daysPerMonth, payloadKB, costPerGB) {
  const activeDevices = devices * 0.30;
  const monthlyUplinkGB = activeDevices * roundsPerDay * daysPerMonth * payloadKB / 1024 / 1024;
  const monthlyCost = monthlyUplinkGB * costPerGB;
  return {
    devices, activeDevices, monthlyUplinkGB, monthlyCost,
    breakEvenUsers: monthlyCost / 0.10
  };
}

function rolloutPhase(phase, criteria) {
  return {
    phase,
    go: criteria.every(c => c.passed),
    gates: criteria
  };
}
```

This question distinguishes senior ML engineers who can think end-to-end—from device constraints to business metrics.

## Why This Is Asked

A practical UX feature tests offline resilience, local storage usage, and simple cross-tab conflict handling without server changes.

## Key Concepts

- Debounce autosave
- localStorage vs IndexedDB
- Versioning for conflict detection
- Cross-tab synchronization basics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal autosave scaffold
let timer;
function onChange(content){
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(()=> {
     const note = { content, lastSaved: Date.now(), version: (NOTE_VERSION++) };
     localStorage.setItem('noteDraft', JSON.stringify(note));
  }, 300);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test offline reload and cross-tab conflict?
- How would you handle storage quota and large notes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical CBA for a local-first search feature, balancing storage, CPU, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing
- Delta sync costs
- Privacy/compliance
- Update cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function indexRepo(files) {
  // naive tokenization
  const index = {};
  for (const f of files) {
    const tokens = f.content.split(/\\W+/).filter(Boolean);
    tokens.forEach(t => (index[t] ||= []).push(f.path));
  }
  return index;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure user time saved and energy impact to validate the break-even?
- How would you design for monorepos and very large repos with incremental indexing?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests ability to design a financially justified, privacy-conscious payment feature that hinges on hardware-assisted security. It requires mapping costs to real-world constraints (region rollout, compliance, energy), and deriving Go/No-Go thresholds with a clear break-even horizon.

## Key Concepts

- On-device security (TEE, secure element)
- Card-not-present risk scoring and decision latency
- Multi-region rollout and regulatory compliance
- Cost drivers: licensing, key management, telemetry, energy
- Break-even calculation and ROI signaling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo on-device risk decision (sketch)
function shouldAuthorizeLocally(riskScore, latencyMs) {
  const threshold = 0.6;
  const fastPath = latencyMs < 200;
  return riskScore < threshold && fastPath;
}
```

